10:46:04 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=305
10:46:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right about the everyday experience: when you’re properly asleep, you usually don’t *notice* pain. The simple reason is that sleep turns down conscious awareness. Your body can still send pain signals, but your brain is less “online” to pay attention to and interpret them. Think of it like your brain putting most notifications on silent mode. If something hurts enough, though, it can still wake you up—like a severe cramp, injury, or toothache. So it’s not that pain completely stops; it’s that sleeping brains usually don’t consciously register weaker pain.
