10:46:16 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
10:46:16 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=464
10:46:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: They’re similar in one limited sense: in both sleep and general anesthesia, you may be unconscious and have no memory of what happened. But they’re not the same state: - **Sleep** is a natural, reversible state where the brain still monitors the body and environment. Pain signals are reduced or filtered, but strong pain can wake you. - **General anesthesia** is a medically induced state that more strongly disrupts consciousness, memory, and responses to stimuli. Even then, anesthesiologists usually also give **pain-relieving drugs** because anesthesia alone does not necessarily prevent the body and nervous system from responding to painful injury. - **Local anesthesia** is different again: it blocks pain signals from a specific body area while you can remain awake. So “completely out” does not necessarily mean “no pain signals exist.” It means you aren’t consciously experiencing or remembering them in the usual way. During normal sleep, mild pain often doesn’t make it into awareness; under anesthesia, the goal is to prevent awareness *and* control pain and stress responses during surgery.
