10:46:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s good evidence that you didn’t consciously notice or remember the event—but not proof that your body had no pain signal at all. Bruises can form from minor bumps or pressure that may not hurt much in the moment, especially while asleep. You might also shift, bump a bedframe, or put prolonged pressure on an area without waking fully. The brain can handle that without producing a remembered experience of pain. Also, pain is not a perfect injury detector: some injuries barely hurt at first, while some things hurt a lot without causing visible damage. So your experience is very common: you can get a bruise overnight and feel like you “felt nothing.” In practical terms, you were asleep and unaware. Biologically, sleep just makes it easier for low-level pain or pressure to be filtered out rather than consciously experienced.
